# Line endings.
#
# The ActionScript row's build output is not a single file: `adt -target cmdline`
# writes a package that is a *directory* -- prog.exe, prog.swf, mimetype,
# META-INF/ and Adobe AIR/ -- and the AIR runtime validates the bytes inside it
# before running the program.  Five of those files are plain text, so with
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default, and set on this machine) a checkout
# rewrites them to CRLF and the runtime refuses to start:
#
#   Security warning: invalid license or package - application may have been
#   modified after packaging
#
# Restoring META-INF/AIR/application.xml to the committed bytes is what fixes it;
# the CRLF forms of signatures.xml and of Adobe AIR/.../Licenses/*/COPYING do not
# on their own stop the package from loading.  The whole bundle is therefore
# checked out byte for byte rather than line-ending-converted, so a fresh clone
# runs the row without being repackaged.
#
# This is deliberately scoped to the packaged directories: the row's sources
# (.as), logs, descriptor input (app.xml) and scripts (build_all.bat, verify.py)
# are ordinary working files and are left to the normal text handling.
exec/actionscript/*/out/** -text
